   "text": {
    "L1": "The Harappan, or Sindhu-Sarasvati, civilisation grew in the Indus and Ghaggar-Hakra river valleys.",
    "L2": "It was not just one or two simple cities. Many important sites show a wide and skilled civilisation.",
    "L3": "Its cities flourished from about 2600 BCE to 1900 BCE, with planned streets, covered drains and standard baked bricks.",
    "L4": "Mohenjo-daro had the Great Bath, and Dholavira had water reservoirs. These features needed planning and organisation.",
    "L5": "People made seals, carnelian beads, pottery, toys and standard weights. Lothal had a dockyard, and goods reached Mesopotamia.",
    "L6": "Seals can suggest trade, but the Harappan script has not yet been read. Historians learn from remains without guessing the signs.",
    "L7": "The cities declined gradually, possibly because of climate change, changing rivers or falling trade. People moved to smaller villages.",
    "L8": "Now check your understanding: which street pattern shows Harappan town planning?"
   },
